content.text:
Yes. The clean way is a TigerBeetle-style state invariant checked at the mutation boundary, plus a targeted assert at the tone-write site. Verified feasibility first: I traced all mutation sites — diacritics only ever write to the last span (guarded by `input_w` 10a) or to `len-2` via autofill (guarded by the seven Q checks), tones only via `apply_tone`/`reset_tone` on the pseudoword vowel range (which now excludes U-after-Q), and literal appends are always unadorned `Span.init(c)`. So the invariant "a U immediately after a Q is always `.empty`/`.level`" holds globally over the effective buffer — it is assertable.

# Updated plan (adds to the two changes already agreed)

## Change 3a — Global structural invariant in `State`

Add a private function and call it at the end of `State.add` and `State.backspace`:

```zig
// The 'U' immediately after a 'Q' belongs to the 'QU' consonant cluster, so the engine
// must never adorn it with a diacritic ('QƯ') or a tone ('QÚ'), in any buffer position.
fn assert_unadorned_u_after_q(self: *State) void {
    const effective_length = @min(self.buffer_length, self.buffer_effective.len);
    var index: u8 = 1;
    while (index < effective_length) : (index += 1) {
        const previous = self.buffer_effective[index - 1];
        const current = self.buffer_effective[index];
        if (previous.equals_ignore_case_diacritic_tone('Q') and
            current.equals_ignore_case_diacritic_tone('U'))
        {
            assert(current.diacritic == .empty and current.tone == .level);
        }
    }
}
```

- Placement at the end of `add`/`backspace` guards every state transition, so any future handler that regresses one of the eight scattered Q guards fails immediately at the keystroke that violates it — including under the fuzzer.
- Cost: bounded loop, ≤ 15 iterations per keystroke; active in Debug and ReleaseSafe (your production mode). Negligible.
- `backspace` can't violate it, but checking there too catches invalid directly-seeded test states and documents the invariant as unconditional.

## Change 3b — Targeted assert at the tone-write sites in `apply_tone`

At both write sites (single-vowel early return and the multi-vowel `tone_index` write), assert the tone never lands on the U of a QU cluster:

```zig
// The tone must never land on the 'U' of a 'QU' consonant cluster.
if (tone_index > 0) {
    assert(!(self.buffer_effective[tone_index - 1].equals_ignore_case_diacritic_tone('Q') and
        self.buffer_effective[tone_index].equals_ignore_case_diacritic_tone('U')));
}
```

This gives a precise failure location (the scan produced a bad vowel range) as opposed to 3a, which fires one step later with the buffer already corrupted. Together with the tightened length-2 assert from change #5, the scan contract is guarded at both ends.

One consequence to be aware of: 3b intentionally uses base-only comparison, so it asserts even for a hypothetical `qư` — meaning the engine *refuses* to ever tone base-U after Q, stronger than the pseudoword exclusion (which only excludes unadorned U). Since we verified `qư` is unreachable, the stronger form is safe and matches your stated intent. If you'd rather the asserts stay exactly as weak as the scan rule, 3b would use `equals_ignore_case('U', .empty, .level)` instead — say so and I'll match.

Before implementing, I'll also grep the existing tests for any directly-seeded buffer that would violate the new invariant (none found in the staged tests, but I'll confirm across all ~4900 lines).

Full change list now: #1 corpus encoders, #5 tightened length-2 assert, 3a global invariant, 3b tone-write asserts. Confirm and I'll implement.

